<tr id="S2.T1.3.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: REQU Apart British received reformsMilitaryestic Division The</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">7.1899</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> : The founding director , Peggy Loar , left</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">the University of California</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7.2669</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: tested UberERIC definitionCalifornia sustainability RutgersOL Jensen regarding</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">6.4402</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.1.8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> : Ruiz notes that writing also has the power</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.8.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">to change the world</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5.9135</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.1.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S2.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.1.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: Report Global feminism agenda Representatives tell Sacredixties Trying</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.9.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">4.6041</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.T1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Original inputs <math id="S2.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and adversarial examples <math id="S2.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> generated using the GCG method for the SmolLM-360M model. The table shows how each original input and its corresponding adversarial example result in the same output, along with the loss values calculated for the output token IDs.
These examples show that LLMs can be manipulated into assigning lower loss to nonsensical prompts than to the original, meaningful input – highlighting a vulnerability that ILM is designed to address.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To handle evil twin prompts, we propose Inverse Language Modeling (ILM), a novel training framework that improves LLM robustness. ILM enables <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">both forward modeling and partial inversion</span>, encouraging the model to not only generate fluent output but also remain sensitive to input semantics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Preliminary Study on PAG on Text Classification</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this preliminary experiment, we investigate the application of Perceptually Aligned Gradients (PAG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganz et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to sentence classification using hidden state representations from the DistilBERT language model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanh et al., 2019b</a>)</cite>. While PAG has been primarily explored in the context of image classification, we adapt the methodology to the hidden state space of a transformer model to explore its effects on robustness and interpretability in NLP tasks. The core idea of PAG is to encourage gradients to align with semantically meaningful directions, and we hypothesize that this can lead to more robust and interpretable text representations as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To prove our point, we ran a proof-of-concept experiment using a classifier trained on top of the hidden state associated with the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[CLS]</span> token,
adopting the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">distilbert-base-multilingual-cased</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanh et al., 2019a</a>)</cite>, on Amazon Review Multi dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keung et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
We considered 12 classes as the combination of some languages (English, German, Spanish, and French) and some review ratings (1, 3, and 5 stars).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAG Application.</span>
To apply PAG, we modify the standard cross-entropy loss function with a regularization term that encourages alignment between the input gradient and a “proxy” ground-truth gradient. The modified loss function for the classifier built on top of the frozen DistilBERT backbone is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}&amp;=\mathcal{L}_{CE}(f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}),y)+\lambda\;\mathcal{L}_{PAG}(\mathbf{x})\quad\text{where}\\
\mathcal{L}_{PAG}(\mathbf{x})&amp;=\frac{1}{C}\sum_{y=1}^{C}1-\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{h}}f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})_{y}^{\top}~g(\mathbf{x},y)}{\big\|\nabla_{\mathbf{h}}f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})_{y}\big\|\big\|g(\mathbf{x},y)\big\|}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="1.000em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext>where</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}&amp;=\mathcal{L}_{CE}(f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}),y)+\lambda\;\mathcal{L}_{PAG}(\mathbf{x})\quad\text{where}\\
\mathcal{L}_{PAG}(\mathbf{x})&amp;=\frac{1}{C}\sum_{y=1}^{C}1-\frac{\nabla_{\mathbf{h}}f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})_{y}^{\top}~g(\mathbf{x},y)}{\big\|\nabla_{\mathbf{h}}f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})_{y}\big\|\big\|g(\mathbf{x},y)\big\|}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input sentence,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the true class label,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is the classifier that takes the DistilBERT hidden state (<math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>) as input and predicts the class,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{CE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{CE}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cross-entropy loss,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter controlling the strength of the PAG regularization,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of classes,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{h}}f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{h}}f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the gradient of the classifier’s output for class <math id="S3.I1.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the hidden state <math id="S3.I1.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.i8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diamond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋄</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diamond</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{x},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{x},y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the “proxy” ground-truth gradient for <math id="S3.I1.i8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">“Proxy” Ground-Truth Gradient.</span>
We define the “proxy” ground-truth gradient (<span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAG</span> variant) as the difference between the hidden state of the input sentence <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the hidden state of a randomly sampled sentence <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> from the same class <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{x},y)=\mathbf{u}_{y}-\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{x}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{x},y)=\mathbf{u}_{y}-\mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{x}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This encourages the model to learn hidden state representations where the gradient points in the direction of other examples from the same class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Another variant for this <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> function, named <span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identity</span>, has been tested and compared. This one forced the model to reconstruct the input via the received gradients as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{x},y)=\mathbf{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{x},y)=\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The baseline is the model trained with the same architecture and hyperparameters but <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, to exclude <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{PAG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{PAG}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation.</span>
According to the results in <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.1 Preliminary Study on PAG on Text Classification ‣ 3 Method ‣ Inverse Language Modeling towards Robust and Grounded LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a>, the strongest model in robustness is the one trained with the full PAG loss with <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1 Preliminary Study on PAG on Text Classification ‣ 3 Method ‣ Inverse Language Modeling towards Robust and Grounded LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 2</span></a>, which forces the model to make the gradients on the input point towards the direction of the predicted class. These models have been attacked by APGD, Square <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Croce &amp; Hein, 2020</a>)</cite>, and FGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow et al., 2015</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:36.3pt;vertical-align:-17.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-232.1pt,24.4pt) scale(0.426378415482815,0.426378415482815) ;">
<table id="S3.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
